Q: Reducing memory usage when writing chunks in order with Go parallel workers A: <p>In your current approach, the buffer map can grow to contain more encrypted chunks than the number of workers, especially if some chunks are slower to encrypt than others. To keep the memory usage constant, you can give each worker its own channel to send the encrypted chunk to, but keep those result channels in a fixed size buffer that maintains the order: a buffered channel of result channels. You only allow a new worker to start if there is room in the buffer.</p>
<pre class="lang-golang prettyprint-override"><code>workers := 10 // as an example
resultBuf := make(chan chan []byte, workers)

go func() {
    for clearChunk := range clearTextChunks {
        result := make(chan []byte, 1)
        // This will block as soon as 10 worker goroutines have started:
        resultBuf &lt;- result
        go work(clearChunk, result)
    }
    close(resultBuf)
}()

// Each iteration below, frees up a spot for a new worker to start:
for result := range resultBuf {
    encChunk := &lt;- result
    w.Write(encChunk)
}
</code></pre>
